Hi Matthias,

* Matthias Klose <doko@debian.org> [2022-06-17 10:18]:
>The proposal is to turn on LTO by default on most 64bit release=20
>architectures. Not proposing to do this on 32bit architectures because=20
>of the limited address space at link time, and up to now nobody tested=20
>LTO on 32bit archs.  In test rebuilds, there were 373 packages=20
>(dd-list in the wiki page) found not to build with link time=20
>optimizations for various reasons.  These range from easily fixable=20
>issues in symbols files to some upstream issues.  The idea is to fix=20
>as many of these as possible, and then change the packaging for the=20
>others to just turn off LTO in the package build.

In one of my packages, turning on LTO did not fail the build, but
exposed a dormant static initialization bug, as LTO apparently
messes with the initialization order [1]. I'd expect more bugs of
this kind to come up once LTO is enabled by default.

Cheers
Timo

[1] https://github.com/isl-org/Open3D/issues/4747

